<#
.SYNOPSIS
    TRMM script check: is the ScreenConnect access client installed, and does
    ScreenConnect have the machine under the company it belongs to?

.DESCRIPTION
    Two things are checked, in this order.

      1. Service - "ScreenConnect Client (<InstanceId>)" must exist. Whether it is
                   running is deliberately NOT checked here: a separate TRMM check
                   already restarts a stopped ScreenConnect service.
                   The instance ID is the part in brackets in the service name. It is
                   passed in as -InstanceId so that a client installed from some OTHER
                   ScreenConnect instance (an old one, or somebody else's) does not count
                   as "installed". If -InstanceId is left out, any ScreenConnect client
                   service is accepted.

      2. Company - The company ScreenConnect itself holds for this machine (what the
                   console shows) is compared with the expected company, ignoring
                   upper/lower case. A script on the machine cannot ask the
                   ScreenConnect server, so the sync job does: on a schedule it reads every
                   machine's Company from ScreenConnect and writes it into the TRMM agent
                   custom field "ScreenConnectCompanyActual". TRMM hands that value to
                   this script as -ActualCompany.

    WHY NOT THE VALUE ON THE MACHINE
      The client keeps the launch string it was installed with in its service's
      ImagePath, and the first c= in it is the company it was INSTALLED under. That
      value never changes afterwards. Proven 2026-10-09 on one machine: the machine
      still said 'Example UK Ltd' while the console said 'Example'. Comparing it gave
      a false "wrong company". It is still printed ("Installed as") because it is useful
      to see, but it is never compared and never fails the check.

    WHERE THE EXPECTED COMPANY COMES FROM
      Normally the client: -ClientCompany. If the machine's SITE has its own company
      (-SiteCompany), that wins. So only a site that is a separate company in
      ScreenConnect needs a site value - every other site, including any new one added
      later, simply inherits its client's company.

    WHAT THE SYNC JOB WRITES INTO THE AGENT FIELD
      the company     the machine's Company in ScreenConnect       -> compared
      (blank)         the machine is in ScreenConnect with NO Company -> reported as wrong
      nothing         the sync job has not matched this machine yet (new machine, or no
                      record found)                                  -> not compared, passes
      So a brand new machine does not alert in the gap before the sync job's next run.
      The answer is only as fresh as that last run.

    This script only REPORTS. It never installs, starts, stops or changes anything.

    ONE SCRIPT, TWO TRMM CHECKS (-Mode)
      TRMM marks a script check as failing on any exit code that is not a success code,
      and an "On Check Failure" task runs whenever the check it is assigned to fails -
      it cannot tell exit 1 from exit 2. So to ACT on one state and only be TOLD about
      the other, the policy needs two checks, one per mode.
      IMPORTANT: TRMM keeps only ONE script check per script on an agent (automation/
      models.py: "Check if script id was already added"), so two checks that use the same
      TRMM script do not both run - the second is silently dropped (seen 2026-10-09).
      This file is therefore stored in TRMM as TWO scripts with the same code,
      "ScreenConnect Company Check" and "ScreenConnect Installed Check", one for each check:
        -Mode Installed  fails (exit 1) only when the client is not installed.
                         Assign the install task to THIS check.
        -Mode Company    fails (exit 2) only when it is installed under the wrong
                         company. Assign NO task to this check, so it only alerts.
                         Put 2 under "Warning return codes" to make it a Warning.
                         A machine with no client passes here: the Installed check
                         already reports that, and one problem should not alert twice.
      Left out (or -Mode Both), the script reports both states in one check.

    Exit codes:
      0 = nothing to report for the chosen mode
      1 = NOT INSTALLED - no client for this ScreenConnect instance on the machine
      2 = WRONG COMPANY - ScreenConnect has the machine under a different company, or none
      3 = the check itself is set up wrongly (unknown -Mode value)

.PARAMETER InstanceId
    The ScreenConnect instance ID, e.g. 0123456789abcdef. Optional - see above.

.PARAMETER ClientCompany
    The Company for the whole client. In a TRMM check, pass the client's custom field:
    -ClientCompany {{client.ScreenConnectCompany}}

.PARAMETER SiteCompany
    Optional override for one site. In a TRMM check, pass the site's custom field:
    -SiteCompany {{site.ScreenConnectCompany}}
    Used instead of -ClientCompany when it has a value.

    If neither has a value (left out, empty, or TRMM could not fill the field in), only
    the service is checked.

.PARAMETER ActualCompany
    The Company ScreenConnect holds for this machine, as recorded by the sync job. In a TRMM
    check, pass the agent's custom field:
    -ActualCompany {{agent.ScreenConnectCompanyActual}}

.PARAMETER Mode
    Installed, Company or Both (the default). See "ONE SCRIPT, TWO TRMM CHECKS" above.
#>
param(
    [string]$InstanceId = '',
    [string]$ClientCompany = '',
    [string]$SiteCompany = '',
    [string]$ActualCompany = '',
    [string]$Mode = 'Both'
)

# Collect every problem rather than stopping at the first, so the TRMM check output
# shows the full picture in one run. Each list maps to one exit code.
$failures = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]   # not installed   -> 1
$warnings = New-Object System.Collections.Generic.List[string]   # company problem -> 2

# TRMM docs: set PowerShell exit codes via $host.SetShouldExit(); exit stops the script.
function Complete-Check([int]$code) {
    $host.SetShouldExit($code)
    exit $code
}

# Which state this run reports on. Quotes are stripped because TRMM may hand the value over
# wrapped in them. An unknown value stops the check with its own exit code (3) instead of
# quietly behaving like one of the real modes - a mistyped mode must not look like a pass.
$Mode = "$Mode".Trim().Trim("'").Trim('"').Trim()
if (-not $Mode) { $Mode = 'Both' }
if ($Mode -notin @('Both', 'Installed', 'Company')) {
    Write-Output "CHECK SET UP WRONGLY: -Mode '$Mode' is not valid. Use Installed, Company or Both."
    Complete-Check 3
}
# Tidy the capitalisation (-notin ignores case) so later comparisons and the output are consistent.
$Mode = @('Both', 'Installed', 'Company') | Where-Object { $_ -ieq $Mode } | Select-Object -First 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. SERVICE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every ScreenConnect client service on the machine, whichever instance it belongs to.
# Listed in the output so a leftover client from another instance is visible.
$allClients = @(Get-Service -Name 'ScreenConnect Client (*' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)

$InstanceId = "$InstanceId".Trim()
if ($InstanceId) {
    $wantedName = "ScreenConnect Client ($InstanceId)"
    $service = $allClients | Where-Object { $_.Name -eq $wantedName } | Select-Object -First 1
}
else {
    # No instance given: accept any ScreenConnect client found on the machine.
    $wantedName = 'ScreenConnect Client (any instance)'
    $service = $allClients | Select-Object -First 1
}

# In Company mode a missing client is left to the Installed check, so it is not a failure here.
if ((-not $service) -and ($Mode -ne 'Company')) {
    $failures.Add("Service '$wantedName' is NOT installed.")
    foreach ($other in $allClients) {
        # Worth knowing: the machine has ScreenConnect, just not ours.
        $failures.Add("Found a different ScreenConnect client instead: '$($other.Name)'.")
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. COMPANY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tidy a value handed over by TRMM. TRMM wraps a custom field's value in single quotes,
# and if the field does not exist or has no value it passes the {{...}} placeholder
# through untouched - treat that the same as "nothing given" rather than as a company name.
#
# The placeholder does NOT arrive with two braces. The TRMM agent starts PowerShell
# without -File, so PowerShell parses the arguments itself and reads {{name}} as a
# script block inside a script block; turned into text that is {name}, with ONE brace
# each side (seen on a real run 2026-10-09: "Expected: {site.ScreenConnectCompany}").
# So anything that is wrapped in one or more braces counts as an unfilled placeholder.
# No ScreenConnect company name is wrapped in braces, so this cannot hide a real value.
function Get-CleanValue([string]$value) {
    $clean = "$value".Trim().Trim("'").Trim('"').Trim()
    if ($clean -match '^\{+.*\}+$') { return '' }
    return $clean
}

# The site's own company wins; otherwise the machine inherits its client's company.
$siteValue   = Get-CleanValue $SiteCompany
$clientValue = Get-CleanValue $ClientCompany
if ($siteValue)       { $expected = $siteValue;   $expectedFrom = 'site' }
elseif ($clientValue) { $expected = $clientValue; $expectedFrom = 'client' }
else                  { $expected = '';           $expectedFrom = '' }

# What ScreenConnect holds for this machine, as last recorded by the sync job.
#   ''           = nothing recorded (field empty, or TRMM passed the placeholder through)
#   '(blank)'    = the sync job found the machine in ScreenConnect with no Company at all
#   anything else = the Company
$actual = Get-CleanValue $ActualCompany
$actualIsBlank = ($actual -ieq '(blank)')

# The company the client was INSTALLED under, read from the machine. Information only -
# see "WHY NOT THE VALUE ON THE MACHINE" at the top. $null = not read.
$installedAs = $null
if ($service) {
    # -LiteralPath because the service name contains brackets.
    $key = "HKLM:\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\$($service.Name)"
    $imagePath = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $key -Name ImagePath -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue).ImagePath
    if ($imagePath) {
        # ImagePath looks like:  "...\ScreenConnect.ClientService.exe" "?e=Access&y=Guest&h=...&c=Company&c=Site&c=..."
        # Take everything after the "?" and read the c= entries in order.
        $query = ''
        $at = $imagePath.IndexOf('?')
        if ($at -ge 0) { $query = $imagePath.Substring($at + 1).Trim().TrimEnd('"') }

        $customValues = @()
        foreach ($pair in ($query -split '&')) {
            if ($pair -like 'c=*') {
                # Values are URL-encoded (a space is %20), so decode them before showing them.
                $customValues += [uri]::UnescapeDataString($pair.Substring(2))
            }
        }
        if ($customValues.Count -gt 0) { $installedAs = "$($customValues[0])".Trim() } else { $installedAs = '' }
    }

    # The comparison. Only when: the client is installed (a missing client is the Installed
    # check's business), this run is not Installed-only, an expected company was supplied,
    # and the sync job has recorded something for this machine.
    if ($expected -and ($Mode -ne 'Installed') -and $actual) {
        if ($actualIsBlank) {
            $warnings.Add("ScreenConnect has this machine with NO company. This $expectedFrom should be '$expected'.")
        }
        elseif ($actual -ine $expected) {
            $warnings.Add("ScreenConnect has this machine under company '$actual', but this $expectedFrom should be '$expected'.")
        }
    }
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Write-Output "Mode    : $Mode"
if ($service)           { Write-Output "Service : $($service.Name)" }
if ($Mode -ne 'Installed') {
    if ($actualIsBlank) { Write-Output 'Company : (none set in ScreenConnect)' }
    elseif ($actual)    { Write-Output "Company : $actual (in ScreenConnect, as last recorded by the sync job)" }
    else                { Write-Output 'Company : (nothing recorded by the sync job for this machine yet)' }
}
# Shown for information only; never compared.
if ($null -ne $installedAs) { Write-Output "Installed as: $(if ($installedAs) { $installedAs } else { '(no company at install)' })" }
if ($Mode -eq 'Installed') { Write-Output 'Expected: (not compared - this check only looks at whether the client is installed)' }
elseif ($expected)         { Write-Output "Expected: $expected (from the $expectedFrom)" }
else                       { Write-Output 'Expected: (no company supplied - service check only)' }

# Installed mode answers one question only. Drop any company finding, as a second line of
# defence, so this check can never fail on a machine that HAS the client -
# the install task is assigned to it, and must not run on an installed machine.
if ($Mode -eq 'Installed') { $warnings.Clear() }

if ($failures.Count -gt 0) {
    Write-Output ''
    Write-Output 'NOT INSTALLED:'
    $failures | ForEach-Object { Write-Output " - $_" }
    Complete-Check 1
}

if ($warnings.Count -gt 0) {
    Write-Output ''
    Write-Output 'WRONG COMPANY:'
    $warnings | ForEach-Object { Write-Output " - $_" }
    Complete-Check 2
}

Write-Output ''
# Say exactly what was and was not established, so a pass is never read as more than it is.
if (-not $service)             { Write-Output 'OK for this check: ScreenConnect is NOT installed, so there is no company to compare. The Installed check reports that.' }
elseif ($Mode -eq 'Installed') { Write-Output 'OK: ScreenConnect is installed.' }
elseif (-not $expected)        { Write-Output 'OK: ScreenConnect is installed. No expected company was supplied, so the company was not compared.' }
elseif (-not $actual)          { Write-Output 'OK: ScreenConnect is installed. The sync job has not recorded a company for this machine yet, so the company was not compared.' }
else                           { Write-Output 'OK: ScreenConnect is installed and is under the expected company in ScreenConnect.' }
Complete-Check 0
